        vm.warp(timestamp);
        // Now check the property: if isOperationReady returns true, then timestamp equals block.timestamp or is less? Actually the bug: P: <=, M: <
        // The general rule: For P, if isOperationReady returns true, then timestamp <= block.timestamp (since _DONE_TIMESTAMP=1). For M, it would be false when timestamp == block.timestamp.
        // But we can only check by reading isOperationReady. Instead we assert that if the operation is ready, then block.timestamp >= getTimestamp(id) > 1? Actually we can assert the invariant:
        // If isOperationReady(id) == true, then getTimestamp(id) <= block.timestamp (by definition) - but this holds for both because getTimestamp is the stored value.
        // The real difference is when getTimestamp(id) == block.timestamp: P returns true, M returns false.
        // So we test: if getTimestamp(id) == block.timestamp, then isOperationReady(id) must be true (P) but M fails.
        // We need to verify that the reference never returns false when timestamp equals block.timestamp. So we assert:
